export function cognitivePage({heading,link,notes}){return heading('RESEARCH UPDATE · 06 NOV 2026','The cognitive core hypothesis.','Karpathy’s proposal, related evidence, and a testable adaptation for this competition.',`<a class="button-link" href="cognitive-core-research.md" download>Download research brief ↓</a>`)+`
<section class="card"><div class="card-head"><h2>The proposal and our interpretation</h2><span class="pill">Research direction</span></div><p>Karpathy proposes separating problem-solving capability from extensive memorized knowledge. We verified this discussion in his 17 October 2025 Dwarkesh interview, around 12:42–13:50; continual learning is discussed around 22:07.</p><p class="source-note">${link('https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/andrej-karpathy','Original interview & transcript')} · ${link('https://x.com/karpathy/status/1938626382248149433','Earlier cognitive-core post')} · Direct X retrieval was blocked; the interview links to that post. The interview is the verified primary source here.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Our competition adaptation:</strong> retain the allowed Gemma checkpoint, retrieve repository-specific facts, maintain concise task state and teach verified tool-use behavior. This is a hypothesis to evaluate; neither a prompt nor LoRA demonstrates removal of stored knowledge.</div></section>
<section class="card"><h2>A grounded repair architecture</h2><div class="core-flow" aria-label="Allowed Gemma model reasons over task-local evidence, uses repository tools and receives test feedback"><div><b>Reasoning policy</b><small>One allowed Gemma model<br>Plan · act · revise</small></div><span aria-hidden="true">⇄</span><div><b>Task-local evidence</b><small>Symbols · source revision<br>Hypotheses · test results</small></div><span aria-hidden="true">⇄</span><div><b>Repository & tools</b><small>Search · graph · source<br>Reproducer · regression tests</small></div></div><p class="source-note">Diagram: proposed design. All inference stays within the permitted model and offline harness. State persistence, compaction hooks and skill APIs still need checks against the downloaded harness.</p></section>
<div class="strategy-grid"><section class="card compact"><h3>1. Retrieve facts at the point of use</h3><p>Read the actual API, caller or failing test before deciding. Cache evidence with a path, symbol and revision. Revalidate it after edits. Keep graph retrieval conditional on a missing dependency or uncertain localization.</p></section><section class="card compact"><h3>2. Keep a bounded evidence ledger</h3><p>Record observed facts, active and rejected hypotheses, last test commands, exit status, changed files and next action. Begin with a 1,000-token working-state target, measured with the actual tokenizer; this is a proposed setting.</p></section><section class="card compact"><h3>3. Resume from evidence after compaction</h3><p>If the harness exposes a supported resume path, reload the ledger and verify source freshness. Otherwise reread it through allowed tools. A saved file is useful only if the agent actually consults it.</p></section><section class="card compact"><h3>4. Learn actions and recovery</h3><p>Use verified training traces for localization, exact tool arguments, diagnosis and stopping. Keep the frozen holdout out of training. Compare behavioral changes against an unchanged no-adapter baseline.</p></section><section class="card compact"><h3>5. Spend extra reasoning selectively</h3><p>Use a bounded second review only for an unresolved contract or contradictory test result. If using a subagent, use the same base model and account for all latency. A second opinion is not independent validation.</p></section><section class="card compact"><h3>6. Keep the competition scope concrete</h3><p>No cloud-oracle calls during offline evaluation. New architectures, Matryoshka weights and online weight updates are outside this implementation plan. E4B must earn its place through measured repair outcomes.</p></section></div>
<section class="card"><h2>Related evidence and practical limits</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>SOURCE</th><th>WHAT IT SUPPORTS</th><th>WHAT TO TEST HERE</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>${link('https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172','Lost in the Middle')}</td><td>Relevant information can be harder to use at different positions in long inputs.</td><td>Vary context size and evidence position; measure repair, not only recall.</td></tr><tr><td>${link('https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08560','MemGPT')}</td><td>Explicit movement between memory tiers can extend effective context.</td><td>Does ledger delivery reduce rereads enough to offset maintenance cost?</td></tr><tr><td>${link('https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.11366','Reflexion')}</td><td>Textual feedback can guide later attempts without changing weights.</td><td>Does a short test-grounded failure note reduce repeated wrong edits?</td></tr><tr><td>${link('https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.24601','Recursive Language Models')}</td><td>External context can be inspected and decomposed programmatically.</td><td>Try bounded inspection first; unrestricted recursion risks the shared budget.</td></tr><tr><td>${link('https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33958','Code-graph study')}</td><td>Retrieval-tool benefits depend on model and task characteristics.</td><td>Compare lexical-only and bounded graph context on both 31B and E4B.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="source-note">These works use different tasks and models. Their results motivate experiments; they do not establish an advantage for Gemma in this competition. This update reviewed abstracts and the primary interview, not full reproductions.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Four controlled experiments</h2><ol class="list-clean"><li><b>C1 · Evidence ledger:</b> best simple baseline versus the same agent with a bounded ledger.</li><li><b>C2 · Compaction recovery:</b> C1 with and without ledger rereading after a supported context reset.</li><li><b>C3 · Behavioral SFT:</b> compare verified recovery traces against a no-adapter baseline after compatibility checks.</li><li><b>C4 · Conditional review:</b> add one same-model review only on a predefined trigger; count all added wall time.</li></ol><p class="muted">Freeze task IDs, seeds and wall-clock caps. Track resolved / scheduled tasks, repeated reads, repeated failed hypotheses, evidence lost after compaction, invalid tool calls, tokens and total runtime. Promote on paired held-out outcomes and reliability, not shorter notes alone.</p><a class="text-link" href="#experiments">Log evaluations in the experiment notebook ↗</a></section>
<div class="grid"><section class="card compact"><h2>Fit within the existing compute budget</h2><p>Use CPU sessions for research, ledger design and trace checks. Run the first scaffold comparisons on Kaggle L4 ×4 (96 GB total, distributed across four GPUs). Use Colab’s 20-unit profiling allowance for a tiny E4B compatibility check; release the 80-unit SFT pilot only after the scaffold is stable and a tiny adapter loads in Kaggle. Keep the 40-unit reserve.</p><a class="text-link" href="compute-plan.md">Read compute plan ↗</a></section><section class="card"><h2>Reusable research artifacts</h2><div class="download-row"><a href="evidence-ledger-template.md" download>Evidence ledger template ↓</a><a href="cognitive-core-research.md" download>Research & ablation protocol ↓</a></div><p class="source-note">Templates are development artifacts. They are not a harness-integrated agent or a verified submission.</p></section></div><section class="card">${notes('cognitive','Record evidence supporting or rejecting these hypotheses…')}</section>`;}
